---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import { BEACON_MAX } from "../../lib/launch.ts";
import { MANOEUVRE } from "../../lib/orbit.ts";
import { dayOf, questionOn } from "../../lib/questions.ts";
import { STATIONS } from "../../lib/stations.ts";

// What Kessler is for (ADR 0018): what a visitor is here to do, and the
// argument the mechanics make. The launchpad says it in four lines; this is
// the long version. The physics is the Kessler syndrome page's.
const stations = STATIONS.map((s) => s.name);
const question = questionOn(dayOf(Date.now()));
const descent = Math.round(MANOEUVRE.descentMs / 60_000);
---
<Base
  title="Kessler: why"
  description="What Kessler is for: say one thing to the people who are here, be heard, listen, and decide what your words cost a sky everyone shares."
  current="why"
>
  <main class="why-page">
    <header class="hero">
      <p class="eyebrow">What this is, and why</p>
      <h1>One sky, and everyone's words in it</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Kessler is a place to say one thing to the people who are here. Your
        words go up as a satellite into a sky everyone shares, where they're
        heard, where they take up room, and where too many of them can ruin it
        for everyone.
      </p>
    </header>

    <section aria-labelledby="do-title">
      <h2 id="do-title">What you do here</h2>

      <h3>Say one thing</h3>
      <p>
        You get {BEACON_MAX} characters and a satellite. Each time it passes over
        one of the three ground stations ({stations.join(", ").replace(/, ([^,]*)$/, " and $1")}),
        its line is read out to everyone who has the sky open at that moment,
        all at once (when several are overhead, they take turns). The stations
        keep a log of what they heard, and each line says how many different
        people have heard it: people, not tabs, and your own listening never
        counts towards yours.
      </p>

      <h3>Listen</h3>
      <p>
        The sky is also a radio. Leaving it open makes you part of everyone
        else's audience, the way their being here makes them part of yours. A
        line passing over a station while nobody is listening isn't heard at
        all.
      </p>

      <h3>Answer the question</h3>
      <p>
        Each day the stations ask one question. Today's is <q>{question}</q>{" "}
        Answer it or say something else; tomorrow there's another, and the
        record keeps how strangers answered.
      </p>

      <h3>Decide what your words cost</h3>
      <p>
        Every launch is one more thing in an orbit everyone shares. A low orbit
        comes round the stations most often, so it's heard by the most people,
        and it's where everyone crowds. When two satellites meet, both break
        apart, and their fragments carry pieces of both lines, broadcast at the
        stations as static. Static takes a turn at the stations between the
        beacons, so a collision doesn't only end two satellites: it puts noise
        made of two people's words in everyone else's way, until the fragments
        burn up. Even the dead satellites the sky keeps carry an echo: the last
        words of one that's gone. You
        can launch as often as every minute, and you can bring one of
        yours down in {descent} minutes to leave the sky clearer.
      </p>

      <h3>Meet someone, the only way you can</h3>
      <p>
        There's no chat and no replies. The only way your words ever touch a
        stranger's is a collision. When one of yours collides, you'll find it
        in <a href="/catalogue/?show=mine">Yours</a>: who it met, what they had
        said, and what the wreck of both lines says now. Neither of you chose
        it, and neither of you can take it back.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="about-title">
      <h2 id="about-title">What it's about</h2>
      <p>
        Attention is a commons. A post costs nothing to make, but each one
        takes a little of something everyone shares: the room to be heard.
        Nobody pays for that directly, so it gets used up. Garrett Hardin called
        this the tragedy of the commons (1968): people each acting sensibly for
        themselves can ruin a shared thing for everyone. Low orbit is the
        newest commons, and it's filling up the same way; Donald Kessler and
        Burton Cour-Palais showed in 1978 that past a point, collisions make
        debris faster than the sky can clear it.
      </p>
      <p>
        Kessler puts the two together, so the cost is something you can see
        and hear: words take up room, crowding makes collisions, and
        collisions turn words into noise made of real people's words, mixed
        with a stranger's.
      </p>
      <p>
        Nobody moderates (beyond a small filter on words) and nothing is voted
        on. Elinor Ostrom (1990) found
        that commons last when the people using them can see the state of the
        resource and what each of them takes. Here everyone can see the sky, who
        launched what, who has been heard, and what each launch went on to cost:
        every fragment traces back to the launches that made it. What happens
        next is up to what each person does with their own satellites.
      </p>
      <p class="ask">What's worth saying, if saying it costs everyone a little?</p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="map-title">
      <h2 id="map-title">How it maps</h2>
      <dl class="map">
        <div><dt>A satellite</dt><dd>a post: something you put into a space everyone shares</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Its beacon</dt><dd>what you say, in {BEACON_MAX} characters</dd></div>
        <div><dt>A ground station</dt><dd>where it's heard, by whoever is there at that moment</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Heard by</dt><dd>an audience of people who were present, not a count of views</dd></div>
        <div><dt>The low band</dt><dd>where the attention is, and the crowd</dd></div>
        <div><dt>A collision</dt><dd>two people's words, broken into each other</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Static</dt><dd>the noise everyone has to hear around</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Decay</dt><dd>time: everything falls in the end, and the sky heals slowly</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Bringing yours down</dt><dd>making room so others can be heard (what it said stays on the record)</dd></div>
      </dl>
      <p class="go">
        <a class="primary" href="/">Launch a satellite</a>
        <a href="/sky/">Listen to the sky</a>
        <a href="/kessler/">What's Kessler syndrome?</a>
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- the three this page argues from; the physics and the real cases are
         sourced on the Kessler syndrome page -->
    <section aria-labelledby="sources-title" class="sources">
      <h2 id="sources-title">Sources</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          Hardin, G. (1968). The tragedy of the commons. <cite>Science</cite>{" "}
          162(3859), 1243–1248.{" "}
          <a href="https://doi.org/10.1126/science.162.3859.1243">doi:10.1126/science.162.3859.1243</a>
        </li>
        <li>
          Kessler, D. J. and Cour-Palais, B. G. (1978). Collision frequency of
          artificial satellites: the creation of a debris belt.{" "}
          <cite>Journal of Geophysical Research</cite> 83(A6), 2637–2646.{" "}
          <a href="https://doi.org/10.1029/JA083iA06p02637">doi:10.1029/JA083iA06p02637</a>
        </li>
        <li>
          Ostrom, E. (1990). <cite>Governing the Commons: The Evolution of
          Institutions for Collective Action</cite>. Cambridge University Press.{" "}
          <a href="https://doi.org/10.1017/CBO9780511807763">doi:10.1017/CBO9780511807763</a>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>
  </main>
</Base>

<style>
  .why-page {
    max-width: 46rem;
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: 1.5rem var(--gutter) 4rem;
    line-height: 1.6;
  }

  .hero {
    margin-bottom: 2.5rem;
  }

  .eyebrow {
    margin: 0 0 0.5rem;
    color: var(--amber);
    font-size: var(--step--1);
    font-weight: 700;
    font-variation-settings: "wdth" 118;
    letter-spacing: 0.12em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
  }

  h1 {
    margin: 0 0 1.25rem;
    font-size: clamp(2.4rem, 7vw, 4.5rem);
    font-weight: 800;
    font-variation-settings: "wdth" 125;
    line-height: 0.95;
  }

  .lede {
    margin: 0;
    font-size: var(--step-1);
    line-height: 1.45;
  }

  section {
    margin-top: 3rem;
  }

  h2 {
    margin: 0 0 0.75rem;
    font-size: var(--step-2);
    font-weight: 700;
    font-variation-settings: "wdth" 112;
    line-height: 1.15;
  }

  h3 {
    margin: 1.5rem 0 0.25rem;
    color: var(--amber);
    font-size: var(--step-1);
    font-weight: 700;
    font-variation-settings: "wdth" 108;
    line-height: 1.3;
  }

  p {
    margin: 0 0 1rem;
  }

  q {
    font-style: italic;
  }

  .ask {
    margin-top: 1.5rem;
    padding-left: 1rem;
    border-left: 3px solid var(--amber);
    font-size: var(--step-1);
    font-style: italic;
  }

  .map {
    display: grid;
    gap: 0.5rem;
    margin: 0 0 1.5rem;
  }

  .map div {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(8rem, 12rem) 1fr;
    gap: 1rem;
    padding: 0.5rem 0;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--grid);
  }

  .map dt {
    font-weight: 700;
  }

  .map dd {
    margin: 0;
    color: var(--ink-dim);
  }

  .go {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 0.75rem 1.5rem;
  }

  .go .primary {
    padding: 0.6rem 1.1rem;
    border-radius: 3px;
    background: var(--amber);
    color: var(--night-deep);
    font-weight: 800;
    text-decoration: none;
  }

  /* as the Kessler syndrome page lists its own */
  .sources ul {
    margin: 0;
    padding-left: 1.1rem;
    color: var(--ink-dim);
    font-size: var(--step--1);
  }

  .sources li {
    margin-bottom: 0.5rem;
  }

  .sources a {
    color: var(--ink-dim);
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }

  @media (max-width: 30rem) {
    .map div {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
      gap: 0.1rem;
    }
  }
</style>
